Day 254/300: Trionic Array I

Problem Statement #3637

Problem Statement Explantion:

An array nums is called trionic if it can be partitioned into three non-empty segments such that the elements follow an Increasing → Decreasing → Increasing pattern.

Formally, there must exist two indices p and q (0<p<q<n−1) such that:

nums[0...p] is strictly increasing.
nums[p...q] is strictly decreasing.
nums[q...n-1] is strictly increasing.

Return true if the array is trionic, and false otherwise.

Solution Approach:

The solution uses a Linear Scan (Three-Pass Pointer) approach. Since the segments must be strictly monotonic and non-empty, we can validate the array in a single traversal:
First Ascent: Start from the beginning and move the pointer as long as the next element is strictly greater than the current one.
Peak Validation: Ensure we actually moved (the first segment isn't empty) and that there are at least two elements left for the remaining two segments.
The Descent: Move the pointer as long as the next element is strictly smaller.
Valley Validation: Ensure we moved from the peak (the second segment isn't empty) and that there is at least one element left for the final segment.
Second Ascent: Move the pointer as long as the next element is strictly greater.
Final Check: If the pointer reaches the final index of the array, the sequence is valid.

Here's the code:

class Solution {
    public boolean isTrionic(int[] nums) {
        int n = nums.length;
        if (n < 4) return false;

        int i = 0;

        while (i + 1 < n && nums[i] < nums[i+1]) {
            i++;
        }
        int p = i;
        if (p == 0 || p >= n - 2) return false;

        while (i + 1 < n && nums[i] > nums[i + 1]) {
            i++;
        }

        int q = i;
        if (q == p || q >= n - 1) return false;

        while (i + 1 < n && nums[i] < nums[i + 1]) {
            i++;
        }
        return i == n - 1;
    }
}

Complexity:

Time Complexity: O(n)
Space Complexity: O(1)



